This repository holds two different kinds of thing, and one license over both
would be wrong about one of them. So:

WRITING is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
License (CC BY 4.0). That covers the posts in _posts/ and the prose on the
pages -- the arguments, the explanations, the copy. Quote it, translate it,
build on it, commercially or not; say where it came from.

    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode

CODE is licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. That covers the Jekyll
layouts and includes, assets/style.css, and the hand-written HTML and JavaScript
that drive the demos -- demos/*/index.html, demos/*/ui.js, demos/*/samples.js.
Apache 2.0 rather than CC BY because CC licenses are not written for software
and Creative Commons says so themselves; matching the license of the projects
these demos are built from was the other half of the reason.

    http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0
    Full text: https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt

Copyright 2026 Grace

Unless required by applicable law or agreed to in writing, software distributed
under the License is distributed on an "AS IS" BASIS, WITHOUT WARRANTIES OR
CONDITIONS OF ANY KIND, either express or implied.

NEITHER OF THE ABOVE covers the compiled .wasm binaries under demos/, or the
copies of wasm_exec.js beside them. Those are built from separate repositories
and from the Go toolchain, under their own terms. NOTICE says which is which,
and reproduces the license text they are redistributed under.
